syntax: parse the argument of zsh subscript flags as a word

After subscript flags such as (r), (R), (i), (I), (k) and (K), zsh treats
the rest of the subscript as a pattern which undergoes parameter
expansion, command substitution and quote removal (zshparam(1),
"Subscript Flags"), as in the common

	${history[(R)${(b)prefix}*]}
	$commands[(I)$cmd*]

The argument was lexed as one raw literal ending at the first ',' or ']',
so expansions and quotes in it were not parsed, a ',' or ']' inside them
ended the subscript early, and so did the ']' of a bracket expression.
It is now parsed as a word, which ends at a ',' or ']' outside of any
brackets within it: the same tokens the arithmetic expression of the
subscript continues with.

Reproducer, before this change:

	$ printf 'echo ${a[(r)$(f x,y)]}\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:9: reached `)` without matching `[` with `]`

and ${a[(r)$x*]} parsed into a FlagsArithm whose argument was the single
literal "$x*".

Part of https://github.com/mvdan/sh/issues/120.

